WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:09.209
Remember that equation.

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:13.680
This we covered in the last episode
of the Cybersecurity Architecture series.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:14.848
Welcome back.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:19.652
This was about security equals prevention,
plus detection, plus response.

00:00:20.186 --> 00:00:22.622
This is basically what we're doing in security.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:26.760
And we talked about at the very beginning of the series some security

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.296
principles and roles and things like that, tools of the trade.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:35.902
Then we moved into prevention
and we looked at each of these different domains: identity

00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:40.240
and access management and endpoint and network and application and data.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:44.677
And this is basically that mostly those controls we put in place

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:49.282
are about prevention,
trying to prevent someone from breaking in or doing damage to us.

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:53.420
Then in the last video,
if you didn't see that, make sure you check it out.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.524
In the last video, we really focused on the detection portion of this.

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:02.429
The thing that's up here
that does the monitoring and things of that sort.

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:08.768
Today, we're going to move on over into once we've discovered that, how do we do response?

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.137
So that's what we're covering today.

00:01:11.137 --> 00:01:15.942
Let's start off with some of the basics of security response.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:23.583
Well, remember also I talked about this diagram where basically the bad guy comes along and he begins his attack by doing reconnaissance.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.953
That's some number of days where they're basically casing the joint.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:30.423
They're looking to see where are the weak spots, where can I get in?

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:35.462
Then the attack occurs and this is where the damage begins.

00:01:36.029 --> 00:01:42.635
Unfortunately, where the attack occurs
and when we're aware of it is separated by a period of time.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:46.639
And that period of time we call the mean-time-to-identify (MTTI).

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:54.481
And the according to the Ponemon Institute's Cost of a Data
Breach survey, which I've mentioned before, this is roughly--

00:01:54.481 --> 00:01:59.119
I'm going to make it approximate --roughly 200 days on average

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:03.923
between when the attackers get in and when we're actually aware of it.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:08.261
Mean-time-to-identify 200 days, the bad guy is sitting in your house

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:12.098
doing who knows what before you finally realize that it's happened.

00:02:12.432 --> 00:02:13.666
That's a big problem.

00:02:13.666 --> 00:02:16.169
But that's what we covered in the detection portion.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:20.106
Then what we're going to look at today is this next bit here.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.408
The mean-time-to-contain (MTTC).

00:02:22.408 --> 00:02:23.576
How long does it take for us,

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:30.383
once we're aware, to get the damage controlled
and the bad guys out and get back to operation?

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.353
And it turns out this is on the order of 70 days.

00:02:33.887 --> 00:02:40.426
So this is the response portion of all of this.
That is clearly not working well.

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:45.565
We in fact, if you look at the numbers over the years,
these numbers stay roughly the same.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:46.933
They don't really change much.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:51.738
So even though we've had more tooling, we've had more understanding, we've had a lot of things that we've tried to do.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:55.708
We've not been, as an industry, all that terribly effective at reducing

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:59.946
that mean time to identify and mean time to contain.

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.681
So what are we going to do?

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:06.753
We're going to look at this this stuff in the response part
and see if we can shrink this 70 days

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:09.289
and make that some number that's shorter than that.

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:14.561
So, for instance, the group that's up here doing this,
I talked about this also in the last video.

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:18.398
This is what we know as the SOC, the security operations center.

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:26.272
And it is a centralized team of people whose job it is to monitor
and look for across all the different ones--

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:30.143
each one of these domains is sending their information up
so that we can see what's going on.

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:37.083
We're going to detect whenever we see anomalous behavior alerts,
things like that, we're correlate all of that

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:41.554
and then we're going to respond,
which again is the focus of what we're talking about today.

00:03:41.588 --> 00:03:46.759
And that response
business has traditionally been called incident response or IR.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:53.433
And one of the big things that can cut the cost of a data
breach is having a good incident response capability in place.

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:56.703
Traditionally, again, that's the terminology we've used.

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.906
It's not wrong, but that's just where we've come from.

00:04:00.673 --> 00:04:05.712
And what it is, is traditionally we've also made this largely
a manual process.

00:04:06.279 --> 00:04:09.782
It relies on heroes and experts, people who just happen

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:13.653
to have knowledge in their heads and a gut feel on what to do.

00:04:13.786 --> 00:04:14.954
And that's how we do this.

00:04:14.954 --> 00:04:19.158
Unfortunately, that really doesn't scale
well, and it's not necessarily all that repeatable.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.295
But their job is to, first of all, triage.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:28.201
That is to when we get these alarms that come
in, we've got to determine, is that a real attack

00:04:28.468 --> 00:04:30.770
or is this just some noise that's out there?

00:04:31.137 --> 00:04:35.975
And if it's a real attack,
is it a significant attack or is it not so significant?

00:04:36.409 --> 00:04:37.343
And which ones?

00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:40.847
Because we never have time to respond
to everything as quickly as we'd like to.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.815
What's the order of importance?

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.884
That's the triage aspect of that.

00:04:44.884 --> 00:04:47.954
Looking and figuring out which patients do we need to see first.

00:04:48.221 --> 00:04:51.691
That's where that word comes from, is in the health care industry.

00:04:52.158 --> 00:04:57.797
And then once we've figured out the pecking order,
then we're going to do remediation.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:01.134
That is, we're going to fix whatever it is, we're going to block.

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:05.505
We're going to shut things down. We're going to apply software patches.

00:05:05.505 --> 00:05:09.575
We're going to put in controls so that we're no longer leaking data

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:13.079
or our systems are able to get back up and running again.

00:05:13.713 --> 00:05:19.652
Now, the more modern approach that we hear, the terminology is SOAR. And I'm going to do my best

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:25.391
to resort to not using any puns when it comes to a word like this, but it's awfully tempting.

00:05:25.725 --> 00:05:30.063
Anyway, source stands for security, orchestration,
automation and response.

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:33.700
So it's automation and orchestration.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:38.037
We'll talk a little bit more about those coming up
and the difference between the two.

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:42.408
But just say where this tends to be traditionally much more manual.

00:05:42.742 --> 00:05:48.014
The idea with SOAR is that we're going to try to make things as much as possible automated

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:53.653
and that hopefully will help us reduce
the time that it takes to contain.

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:55.488
Okay.

00:05:55.488 --> 00:05:58.124
Now we've covered the basics of response.

00:05:58.491 --> 00:06:02.195
Now let's take a look in a little more detail about cases

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:06.165
and investigations, because that's the thing we're going to do next.

00:06:06.232 --> 00:06:08.634
The way this works is we have an attack.

00:06:08.634 --> 00:06:11.304
Remember, over here. Well, now I've depicted it here.

00:06:11.604 --> 00:06:19.412
And this attack then is going to generate hopefully some sort of alarm,
some sort of event, some sort of indication that we have a problem.

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:24.917
And that's going to flow on up here into the SIEM,
the security information and event management system,

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:29.589
which makes up this detection portion of our security architecture.

00:06:30.256 --> 00:06:36.028
Now, we may also have the SIEM feeding in to an XDR,
as I mentioned in the previous video.

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:37.663
Or they may be separate.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:39.932
You may only have XDR or only SIEM.

00:06:39.932 --> 00:06:43.503
So I'm just going to depict it
this way for the purpose of this illustration.

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:51.144
What I'd like to do then, if this system is able to identify that we
in fact think this is a problem and someone needs to go into more detail

00:06:51.144 --> 00:06:55.348
and investigate this,
then one of the things I'm going to do is create a case

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:59.385
and I'm going to create that case here.

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:03.423
Either one of these systems could open the case automatically

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:08.594
in our SOAR system,
the security, orchestration, automation and response system.

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:10.763
So the SOAR is depicted here.

00:07:10.763 --> 00:07:13.599
It's got this case management as one of its components.

00:07:13.933 --> 00:07:16.402
We can also use that to modify cases.

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:18.604
We can use it to assign cases.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:22.442
So I'm going to assign this case over here to this guy right here.

00:07:22.708 --> 00:07:26.879
And he's going to be responsible
for looking at all the cases that are assigned to him.

00:07:27.213 --> 00:07:34.287
And when he does that, one of the things he'll see
is that if we've done a really good job, our XDR or our SIEM

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:41.327
not only created the case but also added in these things right here
artifacts, indicators of compromise,

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:49.502
useful information that the cybersecurity analyst is going to use later
when they start trying to do their investigation.

00:07:49.802 --> 00:07:53.973
So we can take that information
and add that into the case automatically.

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:56.576
So we enrich the case is another way of looking at it.

00:07:57.076 --> 00:08:00.079
And that way this person isn't starting from zero.

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:03.983
They've got some information.
There's a problem. Here's where it started.

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:07.987
Here's some information I have about it. Now we can also with this

00:08:07.987 --> 00:08:13.061
SOAR case management system track and maybe we have an extra dashboard

00:08:13.061 --> 00:08:17.332
that allows us to figure out which cases are open, which ones are high priority,

00:08:17.332 --> 00:08:23.836
who's investigating these and even reassign
these as we need to and make kinds of adjustments of that sort.

00:08:24.604 --> 00:08:27.940
Now, this person is going to need to do this investigation business.

00:08:28.307 --> 00:08:30.610
They're going to have to dig in and figure out what's going on.

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:33.613
How do they know what to do? Do they just start guessing?

00:08:33.613 --> 00:08:37.216
Do they start poking around where we'd hope that they would have a more

00:08:37.650 --> 00:08:41.320
consistent, repeatable way of figuring out what the problems are.

00:08:41.754 --> 00:08:48.094
So we'd like to guide their activities, especially if this person
is not very experienced and doesn't know where to start first.

00:08:48.528 --> 00:08:51.197
So we have these things called dynamic playbooks.

00:08:51.464 --> 00:08:55.868
So a dynamic playbook is something where we have gone in--

00:08:55.868 --> 00:09:00.273
in advance --and determined when you see this, then run this routine.

00:09:00.673 --> 00:09:04.844
If you start here and then you go off and run these two

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:07.513
events, maybe their scripts,

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:12.351
maybe their particular procedures
that a person is supposed to go through and they do those.

00:09:12.351 --> 00:09:16.822
And based upon the results of those things,
then they might do other things.

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:20.192
And depending on the results of that, they do something different.

00:09:20.459 --> 00:09:22.595
That's the dynamic aspect of this.

00:09:22.862 --> 00:09:26.632
So it's not just a static standard operating procedure

00:09:26.699 --> 00:09:30.870
that spells out you to number 1 through 10
statically every single time.

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:35.641
There may be some of those things, but in fact,
what we find is that there's a lot of cases where we have to be

00:09:35.641 --> 00:09:37.577
more dynamic and more flexible.

00:09:37.577 --> 00:09:43.916
And what you get from
this step will depend on what you want to do in the next steps.

00:09:44.216 --> 00:09:46.152
That's why it needs to be dynamic.

00:09:46.152 --> 00:09:51.991
And if we're able to capture that kind of information
in a dynamic playbook, we can guide this person.

00:09:52.024 --> 00:09:57.530
They don't have to have all of the expertise
that knows everything about everything,

00:09:57.797 --> 00:10:01.834
but they can follow the playbook and let it guide their activities.

00:10:02.134 --> 00:10:05.771
Ultimately, they're going to figure out where the source of the problem is,

00:10:05.972 --> 00:10:09.175
and we're going to spell out with the remediation steps
they should take.

00:10:09.375 --> 00:10:14.513
And then they can go back here to this system
and figure out what they need to do in order to get things.

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:17.984
If we're leaking data, stop that hemorrhaging.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:21.554
If it's a system that's down, how to bring it back up and protect it.

00:10:21.854 --> 00:10:25.257
Do the remediation that's necessary with that system.

00:10:26.459 --> 00:10:27.159
Okay.

00:10:27.159 --> 00:10:29.929
Now, we have gotten an indication that there was a problem.

00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:36.669
We created a case, opened it in the case management system, and now we have the cybersecurity analyst

00:10:36.669 --> 00:10:41.040
that's going to go off and investigate
this, figure out what the problem is and remediate.

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.776
Why wouldn't we just automate everything?

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:49.415
Well, I'm going to address that
in this next business of automation versus orchestration.

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:52.652
So think about this. Here's the manual approach.

00:10:52.652 --> 00:10:54.954
And we can think about everything as being on this spectrum.

00:10:54.954 --> 00:11:01.627
Either it's done entirely manually or it's done
entirely in an automated way. And as much as possible,

00:11:01.627 --> 00:11:03.663
I'd rather do it this way.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:09.001
The problem is, in security,
we see a lot of things that sometimes we refer to as black swan events.

00:11:09.301 --> 00:11:11.937
You know, swans are normally white, sometimes they're black.

00:11:12.538 --> 00:11:14.006
It's not impossible.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:15.474
It's just more rare.

00:11:15.474 --> 00:11:17.043
And it's not what we expect.

00:11:17.043 --> 00:11:19.645
And I can only automate what I've seen before.

00:11:20.012 --> 00:11:21.914
Sometimes we even get in security

00:11:21.914 --> 00:11:26.519
some of these things we call first of a kind events.
And a first of a kind,

00:11:26.786 --> 00:11:34.260
okay, I'm probably going to have to figure that out manually because I won't have known how to set up a script to handle that in advance.

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.698
But what I'd like to do is, as much as possible, do

00:11:38.731 --> 00:11:43.335
as much of this as I can in an automated way and for the things I can't.

00:11:43.335 --> 00:11:48.808
I'll stop somewhere along this continuum
and we'll do what we call orchestration.

00:11:49.241 --> 00:11:52.778
Think of this as sort of like semi-automated, where we have a human

00:11:52.778 --> 00:11:57.316
who is directing the system and saying,
okay, go, I'm going to push this button.

00:11:57.316 --> 00:12:00.052
It's going to go off and do these procedures
and I'm going to push this button.

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:01.821
It's going to go off and do these things.

00:12:01.821 --> 00:12:06.559
So think of this also another way
analogy here is the conductor in an orchestra

00:12:06.859 --> 00:12:11.897
who is saying, okay, now I want the violins to come in here
and I want the drums to exit there.

00:12:11.897 --> 00:12:15.034
And they’re orchestrating what is happening in this case.

00:12:15.301 --> 00:12:19.905
So orchestration is a step
that's not fully automated, but it's in that direction.

00:12:20.106 --> 00:12:27.480
And the whole goal of this modern SOAR
capability is to, as much as possible,

00:12:27.747 --> 00:12:33.819
move things in that direction where it's more automated and less manual.

00:12:34.053 --> 00:12:36.522
And orchestration is a step along that way.

00:12:37.089 --> 00:12:39.825
Well, what are the other things that we have to do?

00:12:39.859 --> 00:12:45.965
It turns out if someone attacked us and there was data
that was sensitive to individuals

00:12:46.265 --> 00:12:50.603
that then got breached or got compromised and bad guys got a hold of it,

00:12:51.003 --> 00:12:54.607
then we might have a responsibility here for notification.

00:12:54.874 --> 00:12:57.109
This whole business of breach notification.

00:12:57.576 --> 00:13:01.680
One of the first things we have to ask
is what kind of data was involved?

00:13:01.814 --> 00:13:05.718
Well, maybe there were names.

00:13:05.718 --> 00:13:08.287
There might be Social Security numbers,

00:13:08.287 --> 00:13:14.727
if you're in the US, other types of ID numbers, credit card numbers
and things of that sort.

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:16.328
That's the kind of information.

00:13:16.328 --> 00:13:17.663
So that's the data.

00:13:17.663 --> 00:13:20.299
I need to know what kind of data was compromised.

00:13:20.766 --> 00:13:26.505
Then I need to know what geography were
the people whose data was compromised involved.

00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:29.008
So what nation are they in?

00:13:29.008 --> 00:13:32.444
Maybe even what state or region within that?

00:13:32.678 --> 00:13:39.151
Because it turns out we have different breach notification laws
in different countries, in different parts of the world.

00:13:39.351 --> 00:13:42.488
And even within a country we have different breach notification rules.

00:13:42.855 --> 00:13:47.326
So for instance, one of these major ones is called the Generalized Data

00:13:47.660 --> 00:13:51.797
Protection Regulation, GDPR that comes from the European Union.

00:13:52.231 --> 00:13:57.136
And GDPR specifies very specific, very heavy penalties.

00:13:57.469 --> 00:14:01.407
In fact, if you don't respond within a timely fashion,

00:14:01.607 --> 00:14:08.214
the penalties for this can be on the order of 4% of worldwide revenue.

00:14:08.581 --> 00:14:12.451
So 4% is a huge number for most organizations.

00:14:12.818 --> 00:14:19.058
Or it can be €20 million, whichever is greater.

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:23.729
That's a big penalty if you don't report in a timely way

00:14:23.729 --> 00:14:28.067
when consumers data, citizen data has been compromised.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.703
And again, you might say, but I don't care.

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:34.807
I am in the US or I'm in Australia. I don't have EU,

00:14:36.208 --> 00:14:38.644
you know, really hanging over my head.

00:14:38.644 --> 00:14:39.511
Think again.

00:14:39.511 --> 00:14:44.850
If you've got EU citizen data,
you might be subject to EU rules and penalties.

00:14:45.084 --> 00:14:47.186
So then it's a question of prosecution.

00:14:47.186 --> 00:14:51.123
But regardless, there are other types of rules in the US.

00:14:51.156 --> 00:14:56.462
Each individual state is kind of coming out with their own
sets of rules, with their own set of regulations and so forth.

00:14:56.795 --> 00:15:01.033
So there are lots of these, and this is what makes it really complicated

00:15:01.166 --> 00:15:05.971
to make sure that you have complied with all of these regulations
because there are so many.

00:15:06.472 --> 00:15:10.576
It's really good to have a tool-- point
being --that would help you with all of this.

00:15:10.776 --> 00:15:15.681
Once I realize as part of my investigation
that there has been a data breach,

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:21.053
I'd like to go into the tool and say,
here are the types of data that were compromised.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:24.790
Here are the geographies where it was compromised,

00:15:25.090 --> 00:15:30.062
and here are the different regulatory requirements that I have to
to follow.

00:15:30.462 --> 00:15:35.935
And based upon that, the system would come out and tell me exactly who do I need to notify.

00:15:36.335 --> 00:15:42.942
And that way I don't end up with a bunch of this
because that gets to be very expensive.

00:15:43.509 --> 00:15:47.046
I hope you've enjoyed watching this series
as much as I've enjoyed making it.

00:15:47.313 --> 00:15:48.814
As I told you at the beginning.

00:15:48.814 --> 00:15:55.154
This is a condensed version of a course I teach at a local university.
So you don't get any college credit.

00:15:55.154 --> 00:15:59.792
But on the positive side, you also didn't have any homework
and you didn't have to take a final exam.

00:15:59.858 --> 00:16:02.027
So good news in that regard.

00:16:02.461 --> 00:16:07.232
I hope this also whetted your appetite
and increased your desire to learn

00:16:07.232 --> 00:16:11.136
more about cybersecurity
and that you find this topic as interesting as I do.

00:16:11.804 --> 00:16:14.273
What we'd like is to get some feedback from you.

00:16:14.506 --> 00:16:17.309
If you can add in the comments. What did you learn?

